## sn4.22
sn4.22:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans near Silāvatī. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Sakyans at Silavati .
sn4.22:1.2 | S: Now at that time Venerable Samiddhi was meditating not far from the Buddha, diligent, keen, and resolute. | T: Now on that occasion the Venerable Samiddhi was dwelling not far from the Blessed One—diligent, ardent, and resolute.
sn4.22:1.3 | S: Then as Venerable Samiddhi was in private retreat this thought came to his mind, | T: Then, while the Venerable Samiddhi was alone in seclusion, a reflection arose in his mind thus:
sn4.22:1.4 | S: “I’m so fortunate, so very fortunate, | T: “It is indeed a gain for me, it is well gained by me,
sn4.22:1.5 | S: to have a teacher who is a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha! | T: that my teacher is the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One!
sn4.22:1.6 | S: I’m so fortunate, so very fortunate, | T: It is indeed a gain for me, it is well gained by me,
sn4.22:1.7 | S: to have gone forth in a teaching and training so well explained! | T: that I have gone forth in this well-expounded Dhamma and Discipline!
sn4.22:1.8 | S: I’m so fortunate, so very fortunate, | T: It is indeed a gain for me, it is well gained by me,
sn4.22:1.9 | S: to have spiritual companions who are ethical and of good character.” | T: that my companions in the holy life are virtuous, of good character!”
sn4.22:1.10 | S: And then Māra the Wicked, knowing Samiddhi’s train of thought, went up to him and made a terrifyingly loud noise close by him. It seemed as if the earth was shattering. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, having known with his own mind the reflection in the mind of the Venerable Samiddhi, approached him and, not far from him, made a loud noise, frightful and terrifying, as though the earth were splitting open.
sn4.22:2.1 | S: Then Samiddhi went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. The Buddha said, | T: Then the Venerable Samiddhi approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and reported what had happened. The Blessed One said:
sn4.22:2.2 | P: “idhāhaṁ, bhante, bhagavato avidūre appamatto ātāpī pahitatto viharāmi. | T: (none)
sn4.22:2.3 | P: Tassa mayhaṁ, bhante, rahogatassa paṭisallīnassa evaṁ cetaso parivitakko udapādi: | T: (none)
sn4.22:2.4 | P: ‘lābhā vata me, suladdhaṁ vata me, | T: (none)
sn4.22:2.5 | P: yassa me satthā arahaṁ sammāsambuddho. | T: (none)
sn4.22:2.6 | P: Lābhā vata me, suladdhaṁ vata me, | T: (none)
sn4.22:2.7 | P: yvāhaṁ evaṁ svākkhāte dhammavinaye pabbajito. | T: (none)
sn4.22:2.8 | P: Lābhā vata me, suladdhaṁ vata me, | T: (none)
sn4.22:2.9 | P: yassa me sabrahmacārino sīlavanto kalyāṇadhammā’ti. | T: (none)
sn4.22:2.10 | P: Tassa mayhaṁ, bhante, avidūre mahābhayabheravasaddo ahosi, apissudaṁ pathavī maññe undrīyatī”ti. | T: (none)
sn4.22:3.1 | S: “Samiddhi, that’s not the earth shattering. | T: “That was not the earth splitting open, Samiddhi.
sn4.22:3.2 | S: That’s Māra the Wicked come to pull the wool over your eyes! | T: That was Mara the Evil One, who had come in order to confound you.
sn4.22:3.3 | S: Go back to that same place, Samiddhi, and meditate, diligent, keen, and resolute.” | T: Go back, Samiddhi, and dwell diligent, ardent, and resolute.”
sn4.22:3.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” replied Samiddhi. He rose from his seat, bowed, and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right, before leaving. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” the Venerable Samiddhi replied. Then he rose from his seat, paid homage to the Blessed One, and departed, keeping him on the right.
sn4.22:3.5 | S: And for a second time Samiddhi was meditating in that same place, diligent, ardent, and resolute. | T: A second time,
sn4.22:3.6 | S: And for a second time he had the same thought … | T: while the Venerable Samiddhi was alone in seclusion,
sn4.22:3.7 | S: and Māra made an earth-shattering noise. | T: a reflection arose in his mind … And a second time Mara the Evil One … … made a loud noise, frightful and terrifying,
sn4.22:3.8 | P: apissudaṁ pathavī maññe undrīyati. | T: as though the earth were splitting open.
sn4.22:3.9 | S: Then Samiddhi addressed Māra the Wicked in verse: | T: Then the Venerable Samiddhi, having understood, “This is Mara the Evil One,” addressed him in verse:
sn4.22:4.1 | S: “I went forth out of faith | T: “I have gone forth out of faith
