## sn4.2
sn4.2:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn4.2:1.2 | S: At one time, when he was first awakened, the Buddha was staying in Uruvelā at the root of the goatherd’s banyan tree on the bank of the Nerañjarā River. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Uruvela on the bank of the river Nerañjara at the foot of the Goatherd’s Banyan Tree just after he had become fully enlightened.
sn4.2:1.3 | S: Now at that time the Buddha was meditating in the open during the dark of night, while a gentle rain drizzled down. | T: Now on that occasion the Blessed One was sitting out in the open air in the thick darkness of the night while it was drizzling.
sn4.2:1.4 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, wanting to make the Buddha feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, manifested in the form of a big elephant king and approached him. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, wishing to arouse fear, trepidation, and terror in the Blessed One, manifested himself in the form of a giant king elephant and approached the Blessed One.
sn4.2:1.5 | S: Its head was like a big block of soapstone. | T: His head was like a huge block of steatite;
sn4.2:1.6 | P: evamassa sīsaṁ hoti. | T: (none)
sn4.2:1.7 | S: Its tusks were like pure silver. | T: his tusks were like pure silver;
sn4.2:1.8 | P: evamassa dantā honti. | T: (none)
sn4.2:1.9 | S: Its trunk was like a long plough pole. | T: his trunk was like a huge plough pole.
sn4.2:1.10 | P: evamassa soṇḍo hoti. | T: (none)
sn4.2:1.11 | S: Then the Buddha, knowing that this was Māra the Wicked, addressed him in verse: | T: Then the Blessed One, having understood, “This is Mara the Evil One,” addressed him in verse:
sn4.2:2.1 | S: “Transmigrating for such a long time, | T: “You’ve wandered through the long course
sn4.2:2.2 | S: you’ve made forms beautiful and ugly. | T: Creating beautiful and hideous shapes.
sn4.2:2.3 | S: Enough of this, Wicked One! | T: Enough, Evil One, with that trick of yours:
sn4.2:2.4 | S: You’re beaten, terminator!” | T: You’re defeated, End-maker!”
sn4.2:3.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The Buddha knows me! The Holy One knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, realizing, “The Blessed One knows me, the Fortunate One knows me,” sad and disappointed, disappeared right there.

## sn4.4
sn4.4:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn4.4:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Varanasi in the deer park at Isipatana. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Baraṇasi in the Deer Park at Isipatana.
