sn4.8:2.2 | S: Cattle also bring you delight! | T: One with cattle delights in cattle.
sn4.8:2.3 | S: For attachments are a man’s delight; | T: Acquisitions truly are a man’s delight;
sn4.8:2.4 | S: without attachments there’s no delight.” | T: Without acquisitions one does not delight.”
sn4.8:3.1 | S: “Your children bring you sorrow. | T: The Blessed One: “One who has sons sorrows over sons,
sn4.8:3.2 | S: Your cattle also bring you sorrow. | T: One with cattle sorrows over cattle.
sn4.8:3.3 | S: For attachments are a man’s sorrow; | T: Acquisitions truly are a man’s sorrow;
sn4.8:3.4 | S: without attachments there are no sorrows.” | T: Without acquisitions one does not sorrow.”
sn4.8:4.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The Buddha knows me! The Holy One knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One … disappeared right there.

## sn4.9
sn4.9:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn4.9:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Rajagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the Squirrel Sanctuary.
sn4.9:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn4.9:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
sn4.9:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” those bhikkhus replied.
sn4.9:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn4.9:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, the lifespan of humans is short. | T: “Bhikkhus, this life span of human beings is short.
sn4.9:2.2 | S: We are headed for lives to come. So we must do what is skillful, we must practice the spiritual life. | T: One has to go on to the future life. One should do what is wholesome and lead the holy life;
sn4.9:2.3 | S: No one born is immortal. | T: for one who has taken birth there is no avoiding death.
sn4.9:2.4 | S: A long life is a hundred years or a little more.” | T: One who lives long, bhikkhus, lives a hundred years or a little longer.”
sn4.9:3.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked went up to the Buddha and addressed him in verse: | T: Then Mara the Evil One approached the Blessed One and addressed him in verse:
sn4.9:4.1 | S: “The life of humans is long! | T: “Long is the life span of human beings,
sn4.9:4.2 | S: A true person wouldn’t scorn it. | T: The good man should not disdain it.
sn4.9:4.3 | S: Live like a suckling babe, | T: One should live like a milk-sucking baby:
sn4.9:4.4 | S: for Death has not come for you.” | T: Death has not made its arrival.”
sn4.9:5.1 | S: “The life of humans is short, | T: The Blessed One: “Short is the life span of human beings,
sn4.9:5.2 | S: and a true person scorns it. | T: The good man should disdain it.
sn4.9:5.3 | S: They should live as though their head was on fire, | T: One should live like one with head aflame:
sn4.9:5.4 | S: for Death comes for everyone.” | T: There is no avoiding Death’s arrival.”
sn4.9:6.1 | S: Then Māra … vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One … disappeared right there.

