## sn45.11
sn45.11:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn45.11:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I wish to go on retreat for a fortnight. | T: “Bhikkhus, I wish to go into seclusion for half a month.
sn45.11:1.3 | S: No-one should approach me, except the one who brings my almsfood.” | T: I should not be approached by anyone except the one who brings me almsfood.”
sn45.11:1.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” replied those mendicants. And no-one approached him, except the one who brought the almsfood. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” those bhikkhus replied, and no one approached the Blessed One except the one who brought him almsfood.
sn45.11:2.1 | S: Then after a fortnight had passed, the Buddha came out of retreat and addressed the mendicants: | T: Then, when that half-month had passed, the Blessed One emerged from seclusion and addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn45.11:2.2 | S: “Mendicants, I’ve been practicing part of the meditation I practiced when I was first awakened. | T: “Bhikkhus, I have been dwelling in part of the abode in which I dwelt just after I became fully enlightened.
sn45.11:2.3 | S: I understand that | T: I have understood thus:
sn45.11:2.4 | S: there’s feeling conditioned by wrong view | T: ‘There is feeling with wrong view as condition,
sn45.11:2.5 | S: and feeling conditioned by right view. … | T: also feeling with right view as condition….
sn45.11:2.6 | S: There’s feeling conditioned by wrong immersion, | T: There is feeling with wrong concentration as condition,
sn45.11:2.7 | S: and feeling conditioned by right immersion. | T: also feeling with right concentration as condition.
sn45.11:2.8 | S: There’s feeling conditioned by desire. | T: There is feeling with desire as condition,
sn45.11:2.9 | S: There’s feeling conditioned by thought. | T: also feeling with thought as condition,
sn45.11:2.10 | S: There’s feeling conditioned by perception. | T: also feeling with perception as condition.
sn45.11:2.11 | S: As long as desire, thought, and perception are not stilled, there is feeling conditioned by that. | T: “‘When desire has not subsided, and thought has not subsided, and perception has not subsided, there is feeling with that as condition. [When desire has subsided, and thoughts have not subsided, and perceptions have not subsided, there is also feeling with that as condition. When desire has subsided, and thoughts have subsided, and perceptions have not subsided, there is also feeling with that as condition.]
sn45.11:2.12 | S: When desire, thought, and perception are stilled, there is feeling conditioned by that. | T: When desire has subsided, and thought has subsided, and perception has subsided, there is also feeling with that as condition.
sn45.11:2.13 | S: There is effort to attain the unattained. When that state has been attained, there is also feeling conditioned by that.” | T: There is effort for the attainment of the as-yet-unattained. When that stage has been reached, there is also feeling with that as condition.’”
sn45.11:2.14 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.12
sn45.12:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn45.12:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I wish to go on retreat for three months. | T: “Bhikkhus, I wish to go into seclusion for three months.
