sn45.12:1.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” replied those mendicants. And no-one approached him, except the one who brought the almsfood. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” those bhikkhus replied, and no one approached the Blessed One except the one who brought him almsfood.
sn45.12:2.1 | S: Then after three months had passed, the Buddha came out of retreat and addressed the mendicants: | T: Then, when those three months had passed, the Blessed One emerged from seclusion and addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn45.12:2.2 | S: “Mendicants, I’ve been practicing part of the meditation I practiced when I was first awakened. | T: “Bhikkhus, I have been dwelling in part of the abode in which I dwelt just after I became fully enlightened.
sn45.12:2.3 | S: I understand that | T: I have understood thus:
sn45.12:2.4 | S: there’s feeling conditioned by wrong view | T: ‘There is feeling with wrong view as condition,
sn45.12:2.5 | S: and by the settling of wrong view, | T: also feeling with the subsiding of wrong view as condition.
sn45.12:2.6 | S: by right view | T: There is feeling with right view as condition,
sn45.12:2.7 | S: and by the settling of right view. … | T: also feeling with the subsiding of right view as condition….
sn45.12:2.8 | S: There’s feeling conditioned by wrong immersion | T: There is feeling with wrong concentration as condition,
sn45.12:2.9 | S: and by the settling of wrong immersion, by right immersion | T: also feeling with the subsiding of wrong concentration as condition. There is feeling with right concentration as condition,
sn45.12:2.10 | S: and by the settling of right immersion. | T: also feeling with the subsiding of right concentration as condition.
sn45.12:2.11 | S: There’s feeling conditioned by desire | T: There is feeling with desire as condition,
sn45.12:2.12 | S: and by the settling of desire. | T: also feeling with the subsiding of desire as condition.
sn45.12:2.13 | S: There’s feeling conditioned by thought, | T: There is feeling with thought as condition,
sn45.12:2.14 | S: and by the settling of thought. | T: also feeling with the subsiding of thought as condition.
sn45.12:2.15 | S: There’s feeling conditioned by perception, | T: There is feeling with perception as condition,
sn45.12:2.16 | S: and by the settling of perception. | T: also feeling with the subsiding of perception as condition.
sn45.12:2.17 | S: As long as desire, thought, and perception are not stilled, there is feeling conditioned by that. | T: “‘When desire has not subsided, and thought has not subsided, and perception has not subsided, there is feeling with that as condition.
sn45.12:2.18 | S: When desire, thought, and perception are stilled, there is feeling conditioned by that. | T: When desire has subsided, and thoughts have not subsided, and perceptions have not subsided, there is also feeling with that as condition. When desire has subsided, and thoughts have subsided, and perceptions have not subsided, there is also feeling with that as condition. When desire has subsided, and thought has subsided, and perception has subsided, there is also feeling with that as condition.
sn45.12:2.19 | S: There is effort to attain the unattained. When that state has been attained, there is also feeling conditioned by that.” | T: There is effort for the attainment of the as-yet-unattained. When that stage has been reached, there is also feeling with that as condition.’”
sn45.12:2.20 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.13
sn45.13:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn45.13:1.2 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha … and asked him, | T: Then a certain bhikkhu approached the Blessed One…. Sitting to one side, that bhikkhu said to the Blessed One:
sn45.13:1.3 | S: “Sir, they speak of the one called ‘a trainee’. | T: “Venerable sir, it is said, ‘a trainee, a trainee.’
sn45.13:1.4 | S: How is a trainee defined?” | T: In what way is one a trainee?”
sn45.13:2.1 | S: “Mendicant, it’s someone who has a trainee’s right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: “Here, bhikkhu, one possesses a trainee’s right view … a trainee’s right concentration.
sn45.13:2.2 | S: That’s how a trainee is defined.” | T: It is in this way that one is a trainee.”
sn45.13:2.3 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.146-148
sn45.146-148:1.1 | S: “The radiance of all the stars is not worth a sixteenth part of the moon’s radiance, so the moon’s radiance is said to be the best of them all. …” | T: “Bhikkhus, just as the radiance of all the stars does not amount to a sixteenth part of the radiance of the moon, and the radiance of the moon is declared to be their chief,
sn45.146-148:1.2 | P: evameva kho, bhikkhave …pe… | T: so too …”
sn45.146-148:1.3 | P: aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)
