## sn45.150
sn45.150:1.1 | S: “All the plants and seeds that achieve growth, increase, and maturity do so depending on the earth and grounded on the earth. | T: “Bhikkhus, just as whatever kinds of seed and plant life attain to growth, increase, and expansion, all do so based upon the earth, established upon the earth,
sn45.150:1.2 | S: In the same way, a mendicant develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path depending on and grounded on ethics, achieving growth, increase, and maturity in good qualities. | T: so too, based upon virtue, established upon virtue, a bhikkhu develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path, and thereby he attains to growth, increase, and expansion in wholesome states.
sn45.150:1.3 | S: And how does a mendicant develop the noble eightfold path depending on and grounded on ethics, achieving growth, increase, and maturity in good qualities? | T: “And how does a bhikkhu do so?
sn45.150:1.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops right view … right concentration, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn45.150:1.5 | S: That’s how a mendicant develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path depending on and grounded on ethics, achieving growth, increase, and maturity in good qualities.” | T: It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu, based upon virtue, established upon virtue, develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path, and thereby attains to growth, increase, and expansion in wholesome states.”
sn45.150:1.6 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.151
sn45.151:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, dragons grow and wax strong supported by the Himalayas, the king of mountains. | T: “Bhikkhus, based upon the Himalayas, the king of mountains, the nagas nurture their bodies and acquire strength.
sn45.151:1.2 | S: When they’re strong they dive into the pools. Then they dive into the lakes, the streams, the rivers, and finally the ocean. There they acquire a great and abundant body. | T: When they have nurtured their bodies and acquired strength, they then enter the pools. From the pools they enter the lakes, then the streams, then the rivers, and finally they enter the ocean. There they achieve greatness and expansiveness of body.
sn45.151:1.3 | S: In the same way, a mendicant develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path depending on and grounded on ethics, acquiring great and abundant good qualities. | T: So too, bhikkhus, based upon virtue, established upon virtue, a bhikkhu develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path, and thereby he achieves greatness and expansiveness in wholesome states.
sn45.151:1.4 | S: And how does a mendicant develop the noble eightfold path depending on and grounded on ethics, acquiring great and abundant good qualities? | T: “And how does a bhikkhu do so?
sn45.151:1.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops right view … right concentration, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn45.151:1.6 | S: That’s how a mendicant develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path depending on and grounded on ethics, acquiring great and abundant good qualities.” | T: It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu, based upon virtue, established upon virtue, develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path, and thereby achieves greatness and expansiveness in wholesome states.”
sn45.151:1.7 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.152
sn45.152:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose a tree slants, slopes, and inclines to the east. | T: “Bhikkhus, suppose a tree were slanting, sloping, and inclining towards the east.
sn45.152:1.2 | S: If it was cut off at the root, where would it fall?” | T: If it were cut at its foot, in what direction would it fall?”
sn45.152:1.3 | S: “Sir, it would fall in the direction that it slants, slopes, and inclines.” | T: “In whatever direction it was slanting, sloping, and inclining, venerable sir.”
sn45.152:1.4 | S: “In the same way, a mendicant who develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment. | T: “So too, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu who develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path slants, slopes, and inclines towards Nibbāna.
sn45.152:1.5 | S: And how does a mendicant who develops the noble eightfold path slant, slope, and incline to extinguishment? | T: “And how does a bhikkhu do so?
sn45.152:1.6 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops right view … right concentration, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn45.152:1.7 | S: That’s how a mendicant who develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment.” | T: It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path so that he slants, slopes, and inclines towards Nibbāna.”
sn45.152:1.8 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.153
sn45.153:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose a pot full of water is tipped over, so the water drains out and doesn’t go back in. | T: “Bhikkhus, just as a pot that has been turned upside down gives up its water and does not take it back,
sn45.153:1.2 | S: In the same way, a mendicant who develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path expels bad, unskillful qualities and doesn’t let them back in. | T: so a bhikkhu who develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path gives up evil unwholesome states and does not take them back.
sn45.153:1.3 | S: And how does a mendicant who develops the noble eightfold path expel bad, unskillful qualities and not let them back in? | T: “And how does a bhikkhu do so?
sn45.153:1.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops right view … right concentration, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn45.153:1.5 | S: That’s how a mendicant who develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path expels bad, unskillful qualities and doesn’t let them back in.” | T: It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path so that he gives up evil unwholesome states and does not take them back.”
sn45.153:1.6 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.154
sn45.154:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose a spike of rice or barley was pointing the right way. If you trod on it with hand or foot, it’s possible it may break the skin and produce blood. | T: “Bhikkhus, suppose a spike of rice or a spike of barley were rightly directed and were pressed upon by the hand or the foot. That it could pierce the hand or the foot and draw blood: this is possible.
sn45.154:1.2 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn45.154:1.3 | S: Because the spike is pointing the right way. | T: Because the spike is rightly directed.
sn45.154:1.4 | S: In the same way, a mendicant whose view and development of the path is pointing the right way may well break ignorance, produce knowledge, and realize extinguishment. | T: So too, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu with a rightly directed view, with a rightly directed development of the path, could pierce ignorance, arouse true knowledge, and realize Nibbāna: this is possible.
sn45.154:1.5 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn45.154:1.6 | S: Because their view is pointing the right way. | T: Because his view is rightly directed.
sn45.154:1.7 | S: And how does a mendicant whose view and development of the path is pointing the right way break ignorance, give rise to knowledge, and realize extinguishment? | T: “And how does a bhikkhu do so?
sn45.154:1.8 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops right view … right concentration, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn45.154:1.9 | S: That’s how a mendicant whose view and development of the path is pointing the right way breaks ignorance, gives rise to knowledge, and realizes extinguishment.” | T: “It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu with a rightly directed view, with a rightly directed development of the path, pierces ignorance, arouses true knowledge, and realizes Nibbāna.”
sn45.154:1.10 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.155
sn45.155:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, various winds blow in the sky. | T: “Bhikkhus, just as various winds blow in the sky—
sn45.155:1.2 | S: Winds blow from the east, the west, the north, and the south. There are winds that are dusty and dustless, cool and warm, weak and strong. | T: easterly winds, westerly winds, northerly winds, southerly winds, dusty winds and dustless winds, cold winds and hot winds, gentle winds and strong winds —
sn45.155:1.3 | S: In the same way, when the noble eightfold path is developed and cultivated the following are fully developed: the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, the four right efforts, the four bases of psychic power, the five faculties, the five powers, and the seven awakening factors. | T: so too, when a bhikkhu develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path, then for him the four establishments of mindfulness go to fulfilment by development; the four right strivings go to fulfilment by development; the four bases for spiritual power go to fulfilment by development; the five spiritual faculties go to fulfilment by development; the five powers go to fulfilment by development; the seven factors of enlightenment go to fulfilment by development.
sn45.155:1.4 | S: And how are they fully developed? | T: “And how is this so?
sn45.155:1.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops right view … right concentration, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn45.155:1.6 | S: That’s how they’re fully developed.” | T: It is in this way, bhikkhus, that when a bhikkhu develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path, then for him the four establishments of mindfulness … the seven factors of enlightenment go to fulfilment by development.”
sn45.155:1.7 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)


